      "summary": "Yesterday, Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei published a lengthy open letter saying it was time to \"pace the frontier\" and slow down AI development. OpenAI's Sam Altman and Elon Musk both agreed, publicly voicing their support on X. Even Alphabet's Demis Hassabis offered tentative support for Amodei's proposal. Donald Trump and House Speaker Mike Johnson, however, […]",
      "full_text": "AI News Policy Trump and Mike Johnson think the AI industry is overreacting The Republicans fear slowing down AI development could give China the edge.\n\nThe Republicans fear slowing down AI development could give China the edge. by Terrence O'Brien Sep 13, 2026, 7:41 PM UTC Link Share Gift Image: Cath Virginia / The Verge Terrence O'Brien is the Verge’s weekend editor.\n\nHe’s covered the tech industry for over 18 years and knows a thing or two about synths.\n\nYesterday, Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei published a lengthy open letter saying it was time to “pace the frontier” and slow down AI development.\n\nOpenAI’s Sam Altman and Elon Musk both agreed, publicly voicing their support on X.\n\nEven Alphabet’s Demis Hassabis offered tentative support for Amodei’s proposal.\n\nDonald Trump and House Speaker Mike Johnson, however, seem to think the AI executives are being overreactive and fear that a pause could lead to China outpacing the US in the AI race.\n\nAccording to the Financial Times , Trump said “Look, we’re leading China in AI ... and, frankly, I want to keep it that way, because whoever wins AI, wins.” Johnson echoed those sentiments on CNN , telling Jake Tapper that rushing to place restrictions on AI development could be a “national security threat.” He urged people not to panic. “If Congress just races in and does some sort of emergency session to try and regulate AI, we will lose the race to China.” Follow topics and authors from this story to see more like this in your personalized homepage feed and to receive email updates.\n\nTerrence O'Brien AI Anthropic News OpenAI Policy Politics xAI Most Popular Most Popular LG responds to TV spying allegations StarCraft returns in 2030 as an open-world shooter Your car is selling your data Samsung says ‘Tim Cook’ bought a Galaxy Fold Waymo pulls over, calls cops on riders with a ghost gun The Verge Daily A free daily digest of the news that matters most.\n\nEmail (required) Sign Up By submitting your email, you agree to our Terms and Privacy Notice .\n\nThis site is protected by reCAPTCHA and the Google Privacy Policy and Terms of Service apply.\n\nAdvertiser Content From This is the title for the native ad More in AI OpenAI’s rogue AI tried to hack another company in May Sam Altman says OpenAI going public in 2026 would be ‘ill-advised’ Anthropic CEO says it’s time to pump the brakes on AI Trump is giving data centers a pass to pollute OpenAI just wants to win Lawyer fined $5K over AI-hallucinated witnesses in a murder case OpenAI’s rogue AI tried to hack another company in May Terrence O'Brien Sep 12 Sam Altman says OpenAI going public in 2026 would be ‘ill-advised’ Terrence O'Brien Sep 12 Anthropic CEO says it’s time to pump the brakes on AI Terrence O'Brien Sep 12 Trump is giving data centers a pass to pollute Justine Calma Sep 12 OpenAI just wants to win Robert Hart Sep 12 Lawyer fined $5K over AI-hallucinated witnesses in a murder case Emma Roth Sep 11 Advertiser Content From This is the title for the native ad Top Stories Sep 13 Your car is selling your data Sep 13 The Units’ Digital Stimulation is synthpunk perfection Sep 13 Your Mother Your Mother Your Mother will make you forget all about Marvel’s Blade disaster Sep 12 Trump is giving data centers a pass to pollute Sep 12 OpenAI just wants to win"
